I've been brainstorming ways to improve the multi-view aggregation step in my GSJC scheme for Ego4D-VQ3D. My baseline setup does aggregation without any weighting (w/o Aggregation), and I'm now looking at incorporating a semantic confidence score to weight the contributions from different views/masks.

The idea is: given a segmentation mask $\mathcal{SM}_i$, I compute three probability statistics that collectively characterize mask quality:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{SM}_i|}\sum_{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i}\text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i, \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{\substack{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i \\ \text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i > \wp}} \text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i, \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{max}} = \max_{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i}\text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i,
\end{equation}
where $n$ is the count of pixels exceeding threshold $\wp=0.5$, and the three terms measure mask coverage, high-confidence concentration, and peak certainty respectively. I then combine these into a unified semantic score:
\begin{equation}
 \vartheta_i^{\text{sem}} = \frac{1}{3}(\mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{max}}),
\end{equation}
assigning equal contribution to each component. This is the full semantic weighting variant (P_ave+P_thr+P_max) that I want to test within the GSJC-based multi-view aggregation ablation, where semantic $\vartheta^{\text{sem}}$ and geometric $\varpi$ weights are progressively integrated into the aggregation function.

My current baseline results on the Ego4D-VQ3D validation set, without any aggregation weighting (w/o Aggregation), are: Succ. = 78.13%, Succ.* = 94.32%, L2 = 1.66m, Ang. = 0.76 rad, and QwP(%) = 84.48%.

My question about this setup: after switching from Baseline (w/o Aggregation) (my baseline above) to Semantic Combinations (P_ave + P_thr + P_max), predict the resulting value for each: Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Succ. (in %); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Succ.* (in %); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set L2 (in m); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Ang. (in rad); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set QwP(%) (in %).

I've been brainstorming ways to make 3D displacement estimation more robust when fusing information from multiple views. The idea is a confidence-weighted aggregation scheme, which I'm calling GSJC (Geometric-Semantic Joint Confidence), that combines both semantic and geometric cues when merging candidate frame predictions. Specifically, each candidate frame is evaluated via four complementary factors: segmentation confidence, depth consistency, reprojection consistency, and triangulation baseline quality.

My setup combines all these confidence factors multiplicatively into a composite weight:
\begin{equation}
 \tilde{\vartheta}_i = \max\left(\vartheta_i^{\text{sem}} \cdot \varpi_i^{\text{depth}} \cdot \varpi_i^{\text{reproj}} \cdot \varpi_i^{\text{tri}},\; \epsilon_w\right),
 \label{eq:gsjc_weight}
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon_w = 10^{-6}$ prevents numerical underflow. The idea behind this multiplicative aggregation is that any significantly unreliable factor suppresses that frame's contribution, while frames robust across all dimensions receive full weight. The final 3D position is then computed as a confidence-weighted mean:
\begin{equation}
 [\hat{x}, \hat{y}, \hat{z}]^T = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{N_C} \tilde{\vartheta}_j \cdot [x_j, y_j, z_j]^T}{\sum_{j=1}^{N_C} \tilde{\vartheta}_j}.
 \label{eq:aggregation}
\end{equation}
When all frames are severely unreliable (all weights clamp to $\epsilon_w$), the denominator approaches $N_C \epsilon_w$, so the aggregation gracefully defaults to an unweighted average, which is meant to ensure numerical stability. Finally, I transform the aggregated world position into the query frame's coordinate system via the inverse query pose:
\begin{equation}
 \boldsymbol{\delta} = \mathcal{T}_{\text{query}}^{-1}[\hat{x}, \hat{y}, \hat{z}, 1]^T,
\end{equation}
which yields the VQL-3D prediction.

I'm testing this full GSJC scheme -- semantic plus full geometric weighting -- against a baseline aggregation, using the Ego4D-VQ3D validation set with GSJC-based multi-view aggregation as part of my full EgoHieraLoc pipeline. My baseline, without any aggregation, gets: Succ. = 78.13%, Succ.* = 94.32%, L2 = 1.66m, Ang. = 0.76 rad, and QwP(%) = 84.48%.

My question about this setup: after switching from Baseline (w/o Aggregation) (my baseline above) to Full GSJC Scheme, predict the resulting value for each: Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Succ. (in %); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Succ.* (in %); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set L2 (in m); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Ang. (in rad); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set QwP(%) (in %).

I've been ideating on whether the decision faithfulness signal I use for head-level merge-coefficient allocation should be length-normalised rather than mass-based. My current definition uses total attention mass per region because decision faithfulness is meant to measure evidence contribution to the decision-time representation, not per-token intensity, and I only capture this from total attention mass rather than per-token quantities. But since \textsc{prompt}, \textsc{analyze}, and \textsc{match} regions differ substantially in length across traces, I've been considering an alternative that normalises for region length before aggregating.

My setup is: for each region $r$, let $|\Omega_r(x,t)|$ be the number of positions in that region for trace $x$ and decision token $t$. The density-faith variant first normalises the region mass at each decision token by the corresponding region length, then averages over tokens and calibration traces:
\begin{equation}
m_{\ell,h}^{r,\mathrm{dens}}
=
\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{slow}}|}
\sum_{x \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{slow}}}
\frac{1}{|T_{\textsc{rate}}(x)|}
\sum_{t \in T_{\textsc{rate}}(x)}
\frac{
\sum_{\tau \in \Omega_r(x,t)}
\mathrm{Att}_{\ell,h}^{(x)}[t,\tau]
}{
|\Omega_r(x,t)|
}.
\end{equation}
This uses each sample's own region length rather than a fixed dataset-level length. The density-based faithfulness score is then
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{faith}^{\mathrm{dens}}_{\ell,h}
=
\frac{
m_{\ell,h}^{\textsc{match},\mathrm{dens}}
}{
m_{\ell,h}^{\textsc{prompt},\mathrm{dens}}
+
m_{\ell,h}^{\textsc{analyze},\mathrm{dens}}
+
m_{\ell,h}^{\textsc{match},\mathrm{dens}}
+
\delta
}.
\end{equation}
The $\sqrt{n}$-faith variant follows the same construction but replaces $|\Omega_r(x,t)|$ with $\sqrt{|\Omega_r(x,t)|}$ before forming the analogous ratio $\mathrm{faith}^{\sqrt{n}}_{\ell,h}$. This head-level faithfulness signal is computed on a matched held-out subset of calibration traces from the slow-thinking model, and feeds into the REAM merge-coefficient allocation pipeline, so I'm evaluating these length-normalised alternatives directly against my mass-based definition on the same downstream setup.

My baseline gets, with mass-based faithfulness: on Yelp, MAE = 0.7513 and RMSE = 1.0685; on Music, MAE = 0.5292 and RMSE = 0.8255.

My question about this setup: after switching from Mass-based faithfulness (my baseline above) to the length-normalised variants (Density-faith and sqrt(n)-faith), predict the resulting value for each: Yelp MAE (Density-faith); Yelp RMSE (Density-faith); Yelp MAE (sqrt(n)-faith); Yelp RMSE (sqrt(n)-faith); Music MAE (Density-faith); Music RMSE (Density-faith); Music MAE (sqrt(n)-faith); Music RMSE (sqrt(n)-faith).

I've been developing a way to make head-level merge coefficient allocation in REAM more principled, and one of the three allocation signals I'm relying on is a retrieval criticality signal, kappa. The idea is to operationalize retrieval criticality by measuring how frequently each head retrieves segment-relevant evidence throughout the reasoning trace, since frequent retrieval indicates a head repeatedly restores information needed by subsequent reasoning stages, while disrupted retrieval can cause later generation to drift from the user-item context and propagate errors to the final prediction. Concretely, I follow the retrieval-head criterion of Wu et al., which identifies retrieval by aligning the generated token with the source position receiving a head's strongest attention. Since slow-thinking recommendation traces comprise distinct segments, I evaluate this criterion separately within each one. For an example x, consider head h in layer l, denoted (l,h), at decoding step t of reasoning segment s. Retrieval counts as successful only if both conditions hold: (Cond.1) the generated token w_t matches a token in the segment-relevant evidence region K^{(s)}(x); and (Cond.2) head (l,h) assigns its maximum attention to a position containing a matching token within that region. K^{(s)}(x) is defined by the role of segment s: for USER and ITEM it contains the corresponding user or item context, and for MATCH it also includes the preceding USER and ITEM analyses, since compatibility reasoning should integrate the original user-item evidence with conclusions formed earlier. I aggregate successful retrieval events over the slow-thinking calibration set D_slow, comprising 500 correctly predicted, well-formed prompt-trace pairs from the validation split. To reduce spurious matches, stopword and punctuation steps are excluded before evaluating Cond.1-2, and successful retrievals are TF-IDF-weighted to emphasise rare tokens, giving:
\begin{equation}
R_{\ell,h}
=
\frac{
\sum_{x \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{slow}}}
\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}
\sum_{t}
\tau_x(w_t)\cdot g_{\ell,h,t}^{(s)}(x)
}{
\sum_{x \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{slow}}}
\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}
|K^{(s)}(x)|
+\delta
},
\label{eq:retrieval_criticality}
\end{equation}
where S={USER,ITEM,MATCH}, and t ranges over content-token steps in segment s; g_{l,h,t}^{(s)}(x) in {0,1} indicates whether head (l,h) satisfies Cond.1-2 at step t; tau_x(w_t) is the token's TF-IDF weight; |K^{(s)}(x)| is the evidence region's content-token count for segment s; and delta prevents division by zero. R_{l,h} measures informativeness-weighted retrieval success per evidence-region content token, aggregated over D_slow. Since R_{l,h} is unbounded and strongly right-skewed, with a small subset of heads accounting for most retrievals, I map it to a bounded, comparable weight via a log-z-score-sigmoid transform, kappa_{l,h} = sigma((log(R_{l,h}+delta) - mu_R)/s_R), where sigma(.) is the logistic sigmoid and mu_R, s_R are the mean and standard deviation of log(R_{l,h}+delta) across heads; larger kappa_{l,h} is meant to indicate greater retrieval criticality. My setup uses REAM to merge a slow-thinking model theta_S, which is RecZero fine-tuned from Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct for structured reasoning, with a fast-thinking model theta_F, an adapted TALLRec model fine-tuned from the base Qwen2.5-3B-non-instruct to obtain a behaviourally distinct direct-prediction model (since an instruct-initialised model may retain substantial reasoning and instruction-following behaviour even when fine-tuned for direct rating prediction). Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct is used as the shared base theta_B for both task vectors. I evaluate on the full Amazon Music and Yelp test sets from the Reason4Rec rating-prediction splits, where each instance provides a user's historical ratings and reviews plus a target item, with a 1-5 rating as the label. This is part of a component ablation over REAM's three allocation signals -- retrieval criticality kappa, decision faithfulness, and Fisher update sensitivity d -- looking specifically at how allocation behaves when varying which signals feed the head-level merge coefficient. My baseline with the full set of signals gets: on Amazon Music, MAE = 0.5348, RMSE = 0.8324, Tok = 271.49; on Yelp, MAE = 0.7564, RMSE = 1.0649, Tok = 258.19.

My question about this setup: after switching from Full (my baseline above) to w/o $\kappa$, predict the resulting value for each: Amazon Music MAE; Amazon Music RMSE; Amazon Music Tok; Yelp MAE; Yelp RMSE; Yelp Tok.

I've been brainstorming ways to resolve destructive interference between the structural transport objective and the perceptual guidance objective during training. My approach draws inspiration from multi-task gradient surgery, but adapts it into an asymmetric orthogonal projection. The idea is that the LCFM objective provides the structural transport signal, while the LCPL objective serves as perceptual guidance steering the transport trajectory toward perceptually realistic regions of the target manifold. So when the two gradients conflict (i.e., $\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \rangle < 0$), I preserve the perceptual gradient and remove only the component of the structural gradient that conflicts with it.

Denoting the gradients of the two objectives as $g_{\mathrm{LCFM}} = \nabla_{\theta} L_{\mathrm{LCFM}}$ and $g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} = \nabla_{\theta} L_{\mathrm{LCPL}}$, the parameter update is:
\begin{equation}
\theta \leftarrow \theta - \eta(\tilde{g}_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t) + \lambda_{\mathrm{LCPL}}(t)g_{\mathrm{LCPL}}(t)),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{g}_{\text{LCFM}}$ is defined as the orthogonal component of $g_{\text{LCFM}}$ with respect to $g_{\text{LCPL}}$:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{g}_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t)
=
g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t)
-
\mathbf{1}_{\{\left\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \right\rangle<0\}}
\frac{\left\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \right\rangle}
{\| g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \|^2}
\, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}}.
\end{equation}
The intent is that the perceptual objective remains an effective steering signal throughout training, while reconstruction updates are only incorporated when they don't interfere with perceptual optimization, giving an asymmetric, conflict-free multi-objective optimization scheme.

My setup applies this conflict-free gradient alignment to PCFlow trained on a super-resolution task, using an external perceptual network for supervision — specifically my reimplementation of E-LatentLPIPS. This is in contrast to another configuration I'm also examining that uses an internal perceptual network based on intermediate and final decoder features instead. For the external network setting, before applying conflict-free gradient alignment, my current baseline results on the super-resolution task are: FID = 46.03, PSNR = 23.58 dB, SSIM = 0.6604, and LPIPS = 0.3223.

My question about this setup: after switching from External (my baseline above) to External + Conflict-Free Gradient Alignment, predict the resulting value for each: Super-Resolution FID; Super-Resolution PSNR (in dB); Super-Resolution SSIM; Super-Resolution LPIPS.

I've been brainstorming ways to reduce the destructive interference between reconstruction and perceptual objectives when training PCFlow on a super-resolution task with internal decoder-feature perceptual supervision. The motivation is that although E-LatentLPIPS provides strong perceptual supervision, it relies on an externally pretrained network and may require additional training for dataset-specific tasks. To reduce dependency on external modules, I'm additionally adopting an internal perceptual objective function defined by the model itself. Specifically, letting $\{\phi_l(\mathbf{z})\}_{l=1}^{L}$ denote intermediate decoder features extracted from latent feature $z$, the internal perceptual loss is defined as:
\begin{equation}
L_{\text{internal}}(\mathbf{z}_1, \hat{\mathbf{z}}_1) = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}_0,\mathbf{z}_1,t}\left[w_l\sum_{l=1}^{L}\left\|\hat{\phi}_{l}(\mathbf{z}_1) - \hat{\phi}_{l}(\hat{\mathbf{z}}_1)\right\|^2\right],
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\phi}_l(\cdot)$ denotes per-channel normalized features and $\{w_l\}_{l=1}^{L}$ are weight values assigned to each feature layer. This formulation is similar to the LPL loss except that binary map masking is omitted for simplicity.

On top of this, I'm applying conflict-free gradient alignment to the internal perceptual network. The idea is inspired by multi-task gradient surgery, but adapted as an asymmetric orthogonal projection. The LCFM objective provides the structural transport signal, while the LCPL objective serves as perceptual guidance that steers the transport trajectory toward perceptually realistic regions of the target manifold. When the two gradients conflict (i.e., $\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \rangle < 0$), I preserve the perceptual gradient and remove only the component of the structural gradient that conflicts with it. Denoting the gradients of the two objectives as $g_{\mathrm{LCFM}} = \nabla_{\theta} L_{\mathrm{LCFM}}$ and $g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} = \nabla_{\theta} L_{\mathrm{LCPL}}$, the parameter update is:
\begin{equation}
\theta \leftarrow \theta - \eta(\tilde{g}_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t) + \lambda_{\mathrm{LCPL}}(t)g_{\mathrm{LCPL}}(t)),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{g}_{\text{LCFM}}$ is defined as the orthogonal component of $g_{\text{LCFM}}$ with respect to $g_{\text{LCPL}}$:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{g}_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t)
=
g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}(t)
-
\mathbf{1}_{\{\left\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \right\rangle<0\}}
\frac{\left\langle g_{\mathrm{LCFM}}, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \right\rangle}
{\| g_{\mathrm{LCPL}} \|^2}
\, g_{\mathrm{LCPL}}.
\end{equation}
This way, the perceptual objective remains an effective steering signal throughout training, whereas reconstruction updates are incorporated only when they do not interfere with perceptual optimization.

My setup is PCFlow trained on the super-resolution task using internal decoder-feature perceptual supervision, without yet applying the conflict-free gradient alignment. My baseline results for this internal perceptual network configuration on super-resolution are: FID = 46.01, PSNR = 23.31 dB, SSIM = 0.6515, and LPIPS = 0.331.

