I've been spending cycles on a retrieval-corrected approach to conformal prediction for time series, RCCP, and wanted to lay out where I am with it. The core idea is to build an asymmetric interval from retrieved one-sided residuals, then calibrate the normalized retrieval error with a scalar conformal correction: retrieval supplies local residual evidence, and the conformal correction determines the final scale needed for coverage. Concretely, rather than calibrating raw residual magnitudes, RCCP calibrates residuals after normalizing by the interval that retrieval already produced, so the correction depends on how well retrieval estimated the local scale rather than falling back on a global residual threshold. For each calibration index $j\in\mathcal I_{\mathrm{cal}}$, the retrieved interval $\widetilde C_j$ is computed before observing $Y_j$, and once $Y_j$ is revealed I compute
\begin{equation}
    B_j=
    \max\left\{
    \frac{(Y_j-\widehat Y_j)_+}{\widetilde R_j^+},
    \frac{(\widehat Y_j-Y_j)_+}{\widetilde R_j^-}
    \right\}.
    \label{eq:rccp_score}
\end{equation}
The first ratio measures the positive residual relative to the retrieved upper scale, and the second measures the negative residual relative to the retrieved lower scale, so $B_j$ is a scalar nonconformity score for the asymmetric interval. I then estimate a scalar correction from the empirical distribution of these scores: with $q=1-\alpha$ and $q_n$ the finite-sample conformal quantile level, the correction is $\widehat c=\widehat F_{\mathrm{cal}}^{-1}(q_n)=Q_{1-\alpha}(\{B_j:j\in\mathcal I_{\mathrm{cal}}\})$, giving the final interval
\begin{equation}
    C_t^{\mathrm{RCCP}}(\widehat c)
    =[\widehat Y_t-\widehat c\,\widetilde R_t^-,
      \widehat Y_t+\widehat c\,\widetilde R_t^+].
    \label{eq:rccp_interval}
\end{equation}
Values of $\widehat c$ above one indicate that retrieved intervals tend to be too narrow on calibration data, while values below one indicate conservative retrieved intervals; fixing this multiplier at one corresponds to direct retrieval. My setup is an LSTM forecaster trained per dataset, with observations chronologically split into training, calibration, and test periods with proportions 40%, 40%, and 20% respectively. I train one global forecaster per dataset on the training period and keep it fixed during calibration and testing, using calibration observations only for conformal calibration so that all conformal methods are evaluated on the same frozen forecasts. The backbone uses hidden dimension 128 and batch size 256, trained for 150 epochs with Adam at learning rate 1e-3. Target miscoverage is alpha=0.1, and I'm comparing against representative conformal baselines for time series including split conformal prediction (SCP), online/adaptive methods like EnbPI and SPCI, and localized methods like NexCP, HopCPT, and ResCP, all using the same temporal partitions, frozen point forecasts, and random seeds, with hyperparameters selected on the calibration split when needed. For each test point with reported prediction interval $[L_t,U_t]$, I report empirical coverage, the signed coverage gap $\Delta_{\mathrm{Cov}}=100(\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}-(1-\alpha))$ in percentage points, PI-Width as average interval length, the Winkler score which jointly assesses interval length and miscoverage, and Calibration Time as the post-forecasting time needed to calibrate each method and construct test intervals. Results are averaged over 5 seeds after hyperparameter selection. My baseline (SCP) currently gets: on Air, ΔCov = 0.16 percentage points, PI-Width = 62.72, Winkler = 112.55, Time = 35.28 seconds; on Solar, ΔCov = 1.5 percentage points, PI-Width = 35.24, Winkler = 62.87, Time = 90.14 seconds; on Wind, ΔCov = 0.23 percentage points, PI-Width = 63.42, Winkler = 96.29, Time = 0.72 seconds; and on Electricity, ΔCov = 5.18 percentage points, PI-Width = 0.21, Winkler = 0.25, Time = 0.2 seconds.

My question about this setup: after switching from SCP (my baseline above) to RCCP (Ours), predict the resulting value for each: Air ΔCov (in percentage points); Air PI-Width; Air Winkler; Air Time (in seconds); Solar ΔCov (in percentage points); Solar PI-Width; Solar Winkler; Solar Time (in seconds); Wind ΔCov (in percentage points); Wind PI-Width; Wind Winkler; Wind Time (in seconds); Electricity ΔCov (in percentage points); Electricity PI-Width; Electricity Winkler; Electricity Time (in seconds).

I've been developing a correction step that sits on top of interval retrieval, aimed at calibrating the remaining error of a locally retrieved interval. The idea is that instead of calibrating raw residual magnitudes, RCCP calibrates residuals after normalization by the interval that retrieval already produced, so the correction depends on how well retrieval estimated the local scale rather than discarding that local information and falling back to a global residual threshold. Concretely, for each calibration index $j\in\mathcal I_{\mathrm{cal}}$, the retrieved interval $\widetilde C_j$ is computed before observing $Y_j$, and once $Y_j$ is revealed I compute
\begin{equation}
    B_j=
    \max\left\{
    \frac{(Y_j-\widehat Y_j)_+}{\widetilde R_j^+},
    \frac{(\widehat Y_j-Y_j)_+}{\widetilde R_j^-}
    \right\}.
    \label{eq:rccp_score}
\end{equation}
The first ratio measures the positive residual relative to the retrieved upper scale, and the second ratio measures the negative residual relative to the retrieved lower scale, so $B_j$ acts as a scalar nonconformity score for the asymmetric interval. RCCP then estimates a scalar correction from the empirical distribution of these scores: letting $q=1-\alpha$ and $q_n$ denote the finite-sample conformal quantile level, the correction is $\widehat c=\widehat F_{\mathrm{cal}}^{-1}(q_n)=Q_{1-\alpha}(\{B_j:j\in\mathcal I_{\mathrm{cal}}\})$, and the final interval is
\begin{equation}
    C_t^{\mathrm{RCCP}}(\widehat c)
    =[\widehat Y_t-\widehat c\,\widetilde R_t^-,
      \widehat Y_t+\widehat c\,\widetilde R_t^+].
    \label{eq:rccp_interval}
\end{equation}
Values of $\widehat c$ above one indicate that retrieved intervals tend to be too narrow on calibration data, while values below one indicate conservative retrieved intervals; direct retrieval corresponds to fixing this multiplier at one. My setup applies RCCP on top of two backbone forecasters, LSTM and Transformer, evaluated on the Air and Solar datasets at a target miscoverage of alpha=0.1, and I report $\Delta$Cov and Winkler scores for each combination. My baseline, without this correction applied, gets: on Air with the LSTM backbone, $\Delta$Cov of -1.35% and Winkler of 92.91; on Air with the Transformer backbone, $\Delta$Cov of -3.01% and Winkler of 93.16; on Solar with the LSTM backbone, $\Delta$Cov of -1.66% and Winkler of 26.03; and on Solar with the Transformer backbone, $\Delta$Cov of 0.51% and Winkler of 29.56.

