I’ve been ideating on structure guidance for DMAD. The idea is to amplify the structural signal of the teacher when post-training the student. With \(\hat z_0(\mathcal C)\) the clean prediction of the teacher and \(\hat z_0(\mathcal C_{\varnothing})\) its prediction after the tokens of the structural video are removed from the reference sequence, the teacher score is computed from
\begin{equation}
 \hat z_0^{\,w}
 =\hat z_0(\mathcal C)
 +(w-1)\bigl(\hat z_0(\mathcal C)-\hat z_0(\mathcal C_{\varnothing})\bigr).
 \label{eq:guidance}
\end{equation}
In \(\mathcal C_{\varnothing}\) the first frame and the prompt are kept unchanged. The difference isolates what the structural video contributes, such as contours, layout, and character positions, so with \(w>1\) the student is pushed toward a target that is sharper and more decisive than the teacher itself. Setting \(w=1\) recovers plain distribution matching.

My setup is to post-train the same students without guidance (\(w=1\)), either by updating the four-step LoRA from DMAD or LongLive-Plug, or by self-distillation, which updates the control LoRA with LongLive-Plug frozen. I’m evaluating on the generalization benchmark. My baseline with DMAD start, \(w=1\) (no guidance) gets: appearance/quality (Qual.) 68.00, temporal consistency/stability (Stab.) 91.80, structural control (Ctrl.) 59.20, first-frame preservation (First) 68.10, and automatic total score (Auto.) 71.80.

My question about this setup: after switching from DMAD start, w=1 (no guidance) (my baseline above) to Ours (DMAD start, w=1.5, structure guidance on), predict the resulting value for each: generalization benchmark appearance/quality (Qual.) (in 0-100); generalization benchmark temporal consistency/stability (Stab.) (in 0-100); generalization benchmark structural control (Ctrl.) (in 0-100); generalization benchmark first-frame preservation (First) (in 0-100); generalization benchmark automatic total score (Auto.) (in 0-100).

I've been mulling over a dual-reference guidance scheme that mixes velocity fields inside the ODE solve, with both reference contexts updated in closed-loop from the emitted acoustic patches. My setup is a frozen VoxCPM2 model. Generation has an outer autoregressive patch index $k$ and an inner flow time $t$. Let $H_{X,k}$ denote the base- and residual-LM state, including their KV caches, for reference context $X\in\{\mathrm{FR},\mathrm{EN}\}$. Each context is initialized by jointly prefilling the target text and local-encoder features of $r_{X}$. At step $k$, the LMs provide diffusion-transformer (DiT) conditioning $\mu_{X,k}$, which remains fixed during that patch's ODE solve. Both fields are evaluated on the same evolving latent $z_{k,t}$ and combined with CFG-Zero* with $s=2.0$. Suppressing $k$ for clarity, define $u_{X}=v(z,t;x,\mu_{X})$ and the shared unconditional velocity $u_{0}=v(z,t;x,\emptyset)$. With stabilizer $\epsilon=10^{-8}$,
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
\alpha_{X}
  &= \frac{\langle u_{X}, u_{0}\rangle}{\lVert u_{0}\rVert_{2}^{2}+\epsilon}, \\
\widehat{v}_{X}
  &= \alpha_{X} u_{0} + s\bigl(u_{X}-\alpha_{X} u_{0}\bigr), \\
v_{\lambda}
  &= (1-\lambda)\,\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{FR}} + \lambda\,\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{EN}}.
\end{aligned}
\right.
\label{eq:mix}
\end{equation}
CFG-Zero* splits the conditional field into components parallel and orthogonal to $u_{0}$ via $\alpha_{X}$. The first Euler update is zeroed (fraction $0.04$); the remaining nine follow Eq.~(1). Sampling is fixed (sway $1.0$, temperature $1.0$, 10 Euler steps).

After the ODE produces acoustic patch $Z_k$, its local encoding and projection $E(Z_k)$ advance both LM contexts:
\begin{equation}
H_{X,k+1}=F\bigl(H_{X,k},E(Z_k)\bigr),
\qquad X\in\{\mathrm{FR},\mathrm{EN}\}.
\label{eq:feedback}
\end{equation}
Here $F$ is the frozen base- and residual-LM update. Both contexts follow the emitted history while retaining distinct reference prefills. The open-KV ablation uses independently sampled histories.

The same update supports a conditioning-space implementation: $\mu\leftarrow(1-\lambda)\mu_{\mathrm{FR}}+\lambda\mu_{\mathrm{EN}}$. This variant retains both contexts and Eq.~(2), then uses a single conditional DiT branch plus the unconditional branch. Velocity mixing is used for all held-out test and transfer runs. $\lambda=0$ gives A, $\lambda=1$ gives B, and intermediate values give C. We swept $\lambda\in\{0.1,0.2,0.25,0.33,0.5,0.75\}$ on development data and fixed $\lambda^{\!*}=0.33$ before evaluating the test set; throughout, $\lambda^{\!*}$ denotes this selected operating point rather than an optimized value.

I refer to each compared configuration as an arm. A: real $r_{\mathrm{FR}}$. B: synthetic $r_{\mathrm{EN}}$. C: proposed guidance at $\lambda^{\!*}=0.33$. Cascade: unconditioned English from VoxCPM2, then Seed-VC transfers $r_{\mathrm{FR}}$ timbre on every utterance.

My held-out test set (B1-CNS-test) uses 20 MLS French speakers $\times$ 20 English texts $\times$ 3 seeds, item-matched $n=1200$/arm including the cascade; speakers are disjoint. My current baseline for Arm A (direct cloning with the original French reference) on this test set gets WER 0.016, P(fr) residual French LID mass 0.093, P(en) 0.843, ECAPA similarity 0.601, CAM++-EN similarity 0.624, CAM++-CN similarity 0.746, GOP 4.663, and DNSMOS OVRL 2.9.

