I’ve been iterating on a closed-loop dual-reference guidance scheme that mixes velocity fields at λ*=0.33 (Arm C) and comparing it against direct cloning (Arm A) for inference compute. My setup is a frozen VoxCPM2 on an H100 80GB GPU, generating a 9‑word TTS utterance with five timed runs after warmup. Let H_{X,k} denote the base- and residual-LM state, including their KV caches, for reference context X∈{FR,EN}. Each context is initialized by jointly prefilling the target text and local-encoder features of r_X. At step k, the LMs provide diffusion-transformer (DiT) conditioning μ_{X,k}, which remains fixed during that patch’s ODE solve. Both fields are evaluated on the same evolving latent z_{k,t} and combined with CFG-Zero* (Fan et al. 2025). Suppressing k for clarity, define u_X = v(z,t;x,μ_X) and the shared unconditional velocity u_0 = v(z,t;x,∅). With stabilizer ε=10^{-8} and guidance scale s=2.0, I use

{
\begin{aligned}
α_X &= \frac{\langle u_X, u_0\rangle}{\lVert u_0\rVert_{2}^{2}+ε}, \\
\widehat{v}_X &= α_X u_0 + s\bigl(u_X-α_X u_0\bigr), \\
v_λ &= (1-λ)\,\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{FR}} + λ\,\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{EN}}.
\end{aligned}
}

CFG-Zero* splits the conditional field into components parallel and orthogonal to u_0 via α_X. The first Euler update is zeroed (fraction 0.04); the remaining nine follow the equation above. Sampling is fixed (sway 1.0, temperature 1.0, 10 Euler steps). After the ODE produces acoustic patch Z_k, its local encoding and projection E(Z_k) advance both LM contexts:

H_{X,k+1}=F(H_{X,k},E(Z_k)), X∈{FR,EN}.

Here F is the frozen base- and residual-LM update. Both contexts follow the emitted history while retaining distinct reference prefills. The open-KV ablation uses independently sampled histories. The same update supports a conditioning-space implementation: μ←(1-λ)μ_FR+λμ_EN. This variant retains both contexts and the feedback equation, then uses a single conditional DiT branch plus the unconditional branch. Velocity mixing is used; intermediate values give C. I swept λ∈{0.1,0.2,0.25,0.33,0.5,0.75} on development data and fixed λ*=0.33 before evaluating.

My baseline for direct cloning (Arm A) on this 9‑word TTS utterance on the H100 80GB is: DiT evaluations per patch = 18.0 evaluations/patch, latency = 1.27 s, RTF = 0.474 ratio, peak VRAM = 8239.0 MiB.

My question about this setup: after switching from A (direct cloning) (my baseline above) to C (velocity) — closed-loop dual-reference guidance with velocity mixing at λ*=0.33, predict the resulting value for each: 9-word TTS utterance on H100 80GB DiT evaluations per patch (in evaluations/patch); 9-word TTS utterance on H100 80GB Latency (in s); 9-word TTS utterance on H100 80GB RTF (in ratio); 9-word TTS utterance on H100 80GB Peak VRAM (in MiB).

I’ve been iterating on a storage-budget view of in-context learning. The idea is that fine-tuning replaces the context by a fixed state. Let \(S\) be any such state built from \(D\), with randomness independent of the sampled in-context outputs: fine-tuned weights, an adapter, or a compiled or compressed replacement of the context memory. Let any decoder \(p_S(\cdot\mid x)\) read the state, and let a workload of \(K\) inputs \(x_1,\dots,x_K\) have in-context outputs \(\hat Y_j\sim p_A(\cdot\mid D,x_j)\), sampled independently given \(D\).

\begin{theorem}[Storage budget]\label{thm:storage}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:storage}
\sum_{j=1}^{K}\E\,\KL\big(p_A(\cdot\mid D,x_j)\,\big\|\,p_S(\cdot\mid x_j)\big)\;\ge\;I(\hat Y_{1:K};D)-I(D;S),
\end{equation}
and \(I(D;S)\le H(S)\) when \(S\) takes finitely many values.
\end{theorem}

A state that reproduces the context memory on the workload to within \(\varepsilon\) bits in total stores at least \(I(\hat Y_{1:K};D)-\varepsilon\) bits about \(D\), so no fine-tune whose capacity bound is below \(I(\hat Y_{1:K};D)-\varepsilon\) can reproduce it that closely.

The information can be estimated from the model alone, by resampling the content of \(D\) and scoring the in-context outputs under every variant. My setup is GPT-2 on tables of facts. The context is a table of four facts, each a fixed key of three tokens followed by one of eight candidate tokens, so it ranges over \(8^4=4{,}096\) tables and holds 12 bits. The workload is the four keys, and an output is GPT-2’s next token after a key, read as one of the candidates or as none of them.

I estimate the information from the model alone. Resample the content of \(D\) into \(M\) variants \(D^{(1)},\dots,D^{(M)}\), sample in-context outputs under each variant and score them under every variant; for \(D\) drawn uniformly from the variants, and with \(p_A(\hat Y_{1:K}\mid D)=\prod_jp_A(\hat Y_j\mid D,x_j)\),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Ihat}
I(\hat Y_{1:K};D)=\frac1M\sum_{m=1}^{M}\E_{\hat Y\sim p_A(\cdot\mid D^{(m)})}\log_2\frac{p_A(\hat Y\mid D^{(m)})}{\frac1M\sum_{m'=1}^{M}p_A(\hat Y\mid D^{(m')})}.
\end{equation}
With the variants drawn from the data distribution, the expectation of the right side over the draws is a lower bound on \(I(\hat Y_{1:K};D)\) that saturates at \(\log_2M\) bits, so the estimate certifies the budget for states that store fewer bits than that. This is the information budget of \citet{chlon2026predictable}, computed over content-resampled datasets.

My current baseline using Equation \eqref{eq:Ihat} with \(M=512\) tables drawn at random gives an estimated \(I(\hat Y;D)\) of 4.93 bits.

My question about this setup: after switching from Estimated I(Ŷ;D) via resampled contexts (Eq. Ihat, M=512) (my baseline above) to Exact I(Ŷ;D) from all 4,096 tables, predict the resulting value for each: GPT-2 on tables of facts estimated I(Ŷ;D) vs exact I(Ŷ;D) (in bits).

I’ve been working on auxiliary reward shaping for the BIRD-RL training curriculum, specifically looking at whether denser signals can help when the execution-based reward alone gives no positive feedback. One of the designs I’m trying is a heuristic rubric-based reward. The idea is to add a small shaping signal only for trajectories whose execution test pass rate is zero; any trajectory that passes at least one test case is still optimized primarily by the execution-based reward.

My setup is this: the rubric-based reward has two criteria. The first is whether the submitted SQL executes successfully. The second is whether the model identifies known bugs in its reasoning. For the bug-identification part, I match the model’s reasoning against a pre-extracted bug checklist using an LLM verifier. The reward is computed as

\begin{equation}
R_{\mathrm{rubric}}
=
0.1 \times \mathbb{I}_{\mathrm{exec}}
+
0.1 \times
\frac{N_{\mathrm{bug}}^{\mathrm{id}}}{N_{\mathrm{bug}}}.
\end{equation}

Here, \(\mathbb{I}_{\mathrm{exec}}\) indicates whether the submitted SQL executes without syntax errors, \(N_{\mathrm{bug}}\) is the number of known bugs in the checklist, and \(N_{\mathrm{bug}}^{\mathrm{id}}\) is the number of bugs identified by the model. The prompts for bug extraction and verification are the ones I have in Figure \ref{fig:rubric_extract_prompt} and \ref{fig:rubric_verify_prompt}.

Right now, with BIRD-Talon-7B using only the execution-based reward, my baselines on BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite are: Quer. Success Rate 46.13%, Mana. Success Rate 45.33%, Pers. Success Rate 40.43%, and Overall Success Rate 44.4%.

My question about this setup: after switching from BIRD-Talon-7B (execution-based reward only) (my baseline above) to (f) w/ Heuristic Rubric, predict the resulting value for each: BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite (Quer.) Success Rate (SR%) (in %); BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite (Mana.) Success Rate (SR%) (in %); BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite (Pers.) Success Rate (SR%) (in %); BIRD-CRITIC-SQLite (Overall) Success Rate (SR%) (in %).
